"[2610.05744v1] CIPHER-MoE: Balancing Efficiency and Routing Fidelity in Trillion-Scale MoE Training (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05744v1)\nciteturn41360view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05744v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 5 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:CIPHER-MoE: Balancing Efficiency and Routing Fidelity in Trillion-Scale MoE Training\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jing Li , cite6†Jian Meng , cite7†Yingmeng Gao , cite8†Suming Qiu , cite9†Linyuan Qiu , cite10†Dongfang Li , cite11†Baotian Hu , cite12†Binfan Zheng , cite13†Rongqian Zhao , cite14†Weijian Sun , cite15†Xin Chen L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled CIPHER-MoE: Balancing Efficiency and Routing Fidelity in Trillion-Scale MoE Training, by Jing Li and 10 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) has been widely adopted in recent large language model (LLM) architectures. However, scaling up MoE in LLM training introduces system-level challenges on training, where non-uniform token routing can lead to highly imbalanced workloads across experts and devices, further destabilizing the training process.\nL17: With trillion-scale LLMs, imbalanced expert workloads further amplify the resource cost of MoE training, resulting in degraded training efficiency and hardware utilization for underloaded experts, while hot experts require additional resources to accommodate excessive workloads. Recent studies address imbalanced MoE training through intricate parallelism strategies or resource reallocation.\nL18: However, these system-level approaches often introduce additional resource requirements and considerable orchestration complexity, which become increasingly difficult to afford when training trillion-parameter LLMs under constrained computational resources. This work introduces CIPHER-MoE, which mitigates MoE workload imbalance while keeping the router's token-side Top-K selection unchanged.\nL19: CIPHER-MoE applies affinity-aware Expert-to-Token filtering with explicit capacity control to reduce hotspot expert workloads without additional hardware resources or complex runtime design. The proposed method has been evaluated on large-scale MoE models, including DeepSeek-V4-Pro, showing up to 64.9 percentage points Top-1 expert workload reduction and 1.10$\\times$-1.94$\\times$ training acceleration, while preserving the training quality. The source code will be released soon.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2610.05744 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2610.05744v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.05744†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jing Li [cite21†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 5 Oct 2026 03:51:17 UTC (7,346 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled CIPHER-MoE: Balancing Efficiency and Routing Fidelity in Trillion-Scale MoE Training, by Jing Li and 10 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite16†View PDF L36:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite22†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite23†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L45: \nL46: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2026-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite29†cs L51: cite30†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:  